🐛 Bug
We have several binaries that use libtorch and a small number of them have started to segfault during shutdown. These segfaults are intermittent and happen at different rates when running different models.
A detailed description of the issue, the cause, and a proposed fix are included below.
Description of Issue
Most of the backtraces we got weren't very helpful, but a few included some useful information:
0x00007fffa701ac58 in std::default_delete<at::cuda::(anonymous namespace)::DeviceThreadHandlePool<cudnnContext*, &at::native::(anonymous namespace)::createCuDNNHandle, &at::native::(anonymous namespace)::destroyCuDNNHandle>::PoolWindow>::operator()(at::cuda::(anonymous namespace)::DeviceThreadHandlePool<cudnnContext*, &at::native::(anonymous namespace)::createCuDNNHandle, &at::native::(anonymous namespace)::destroyCuDNNHandle>::PoolWindow*) const ()

0x00007fffa700ec38 in std::default_delete<at::cuda::(anonymous namespace)::DeviceThreadHandlePool<cublasContext*, &at::cuda::(anonymous namespace)::createCublasHandle, &at::cuda::(anonymous namespace)::destroyCublasHandle>::PoolWindow>::operator()(at::cuda::(anonymous namespace)::DeviceThreadHandlePool<cublasContext*, &at::cuda::(anonymous namespace)::createCublasHandle, &at::cuda::(anonymous namespace)::destroyCublasHandle>::PoolWindow*) const ()

These appear to correspond to the destruction of myPoolWindow in the below snippets:



      pytorch/aten/src/ATen/cudnn/Handle.cpp


        Lines 25 to 47
      in
      8cb1950







           at::cuda::DeviceThreadHandlePool<cudnnHandle_t, createCuDNNHandle, destroyCuDNNHandle> pool; 









           // Thread local PoolWindows are wrapped by unique_ptrs and lazily-initialized 




           // to avoid initialization issues that caused hangs on Windows. 




           // See: https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/22405 




           // This thread local unique_ptrs will be destroyed when the thread terminates, 




           // releasing its reserved handles back to the pool. 




           thread_local std::unique_ptr<decltype(pool)::PoolWindow> myPoolWindow; 









           } // namespace 









           cudnnHandle_t getCudnnHandle() 




           { 




             int device; 




             AT_CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetDevice(&device)); 









             if (!myPoolWindow) 




               myPoolWindow.reset(pool.newPoolWindow()); 









             auto handle = myPoolWindow->reserve(device); 




             AT_CUDNN_CHECK(cudnnSetStream(handle, c10::cuda::getCurrentCUDAStream())); 




             return handle; 




           } 








      pytorch/aten/src/ATen/cuda/CublasHandlePool.cpp


        Lines 24 to 45
      in
      8cb1950







           DeviceThreadHandlePool<cublasHandle_t, createCublasHandle, destroyCublasHandle> pool; 









           // Thread local PoolWindows are wrapped by unique_ptrs and lazily-initialized 




           // to avoid initialization issues that caused hangs on Windows. 




           // See: https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/22405 




           // This thread local unique_ptrs will be destroyed when the thread terminates, 




           // releasing its reserved handles back to the pool. 




           thread_local std::unique_ptr<decltype(pool)::PoolWindow> myPoolWindow; 









           } // namespace 









           cublasHandle_t getCurrentCUDABlasHandle() { 




             int device; 




             AT_CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetDevice(&device)); 









             if (!myPoolWindow) 




               myPoolWindow.reset(pool.newPoolWindow()); 




             auto handle = myPoolWindow->reserve(device); 




             auto stream = c10::cuda::getCurrentCUDAStream(); 




             TORCH_CUDABLAS_CHECK(cublasSetStream(handle, stream)); 




             return handle; 




           } 





Cause of the segfault
The cause of the segfault is a combination of a few things:

PoolWindow is initialized with a reference to a DeviceThreadHandlePool (pool in the snippets above) and it attempts to use this reference during destruction:




      pytorch/aten/src/ATen/cuda/detail/DeviceThreadHandles.h


        Lines 83 to 88
      in
      8cb1950







           class PoolWindow 




           { 




           DeviceThreadHandlePool &parent; 




           public: 




           PoolWindow(DeviceThreadHandlePool &parent): parent(parent) {} 




           ~PoolWindow(){ release(); } 








      pytorch/aten/src/ATen/cuda/detail/DeviceThreadHandles.h


        Lines 120 to 127
      in
      8cb1950







           // Called by the destructor.  Releases this thread's handles back into the pool. 




           void release() { 




               if(my_handles.size() > 0) { 




                   std::lock_guard<std::mutex> guard(parent.mutex); 




                   for(auto d_h : my_handles) 




                       parent.available_handles[d_h.first].push_back(d_h.second); 




               } 




           } 






PoolWindow can outlive the DeviceThreadHandlePool it was initialized with.

In the snippets at the top of this issue, myPoolWindow is defined as thread_local. This means one instance will be created per thread that calls getCudnnHandle() or getCurrentCUDABlasHandle()
The problem is that thread_local objects are destroyed when the calling thread exits.
Since it's possible for a thread to be defined in another translation unit and initialization/destruction order across translation units is unspecified, it is possible for calling threads to exit after pool is destroyed.
This means that myPoolWindow could be destroyed after pool, causing release() to use an invalid reference.
Proposed Fix
Refactor DeviceThreadHandlePool to inherit from std::enable_shared_from_this and pass a shared pointer into to the PoolWindow constructor instead of a reference.
This fixed the issue in our internal build of Torch. Happy to put up a PR if you're okay with this fix.
Note: this issue also exists in aten/src/ATen/cuda/CuSparseHandlePool.cpp although we didn't see any segfaults there in our applications.
